00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.000
Engine and the CI/CD pipelines
from the data protection

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.290
toolkit to facilitate and to
simplify the whole process.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.760
What is Terraform Engine?

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.150
Terraform Engine is a framework
to jump start your organization

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.230
onto GCP.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.480
It is an open source
Terraform module generator

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.250
that puts together
Terraform resources

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.500
into complete
deployments with values

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.620
that are specific to
your infrastructure.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.440
Terraform Engine offers
different types of recipes.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.090
And here, I'm demonstrating
the foundational recipe,

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.390
which is the most
common based IPs

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.270
that health care focused GCP
infrastructure should usually

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:47.570
set up.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.590
So what is a foundation recipe?

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:53.800
Foundation recipe helps you
set up a structure like this.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.410
It will help you set up
a DevOps project used

00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:00.490
for remote Terraform state
management CI/CD resources

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:02.560
to test and deploy
your Terraform

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.180
configs automatically.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.760
It will also set
up a Audit project

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.750
which is used to host two
audit log sinks, a short term

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.760
one-year BigQuery
log sink and also

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.510
a long-term seven-year
Cloud Storage log sink.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.630
And in addition, the
foundation recipe

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.740
also set up a
monitoring project,

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.220
which is used to host the
Forseti infrastructure.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:27.940
And Forseti is used to scan and
monitor your GCP organization

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:31.520
for potential security
policy violations.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.390
So this is actually a
Terraform Engine config,

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.280
and now we'll use this
to generate the Terraform

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.460
configs using Terraform Engine.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.870
And so here, you can see
I'm specifying the recipe

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:47.050
to be the foundation recipe from
the default templates offered

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.430
by Terraform Engine.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.550
And in each of the data
block, I can specify

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.690
details for each component.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:55.720
So for example,
for DevOps project,

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:00.550
I specified the project ID
to be dpt-demo-1-devops.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.000
And similarly, for Audit,
I can specify the name

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.610
or the project ID for
the project itself.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.639
And I can also
specify the set name

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:13.900
and also bucket name
for the audit log sinks.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.560
And note that we also deliver
a setup default organization

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.210
policies which is based
on best practices,

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.270
and it's a good setup
organizing policies

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:26.260
that you can use to start
with in your organization.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.470
And also, here we
have the CI/CD block.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.770
This block will configure
the CI/CD components

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.520
which will set, for example,
which GitHub repo to monitor,

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.840
which branch to monitor
to detect changes, push

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.140
to that branch, and auto-deploy
the changes in that branch

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:47.450
to your GCP infrastructure.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.240
And it also allows set
additional, for example,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.780
project owners to a specific
group and things like those.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.810
And also, note that there
is a disabled block,

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.300
and there's one attribute
set to true, which

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.000
is a bootstrap GCS backend.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.390
So the meaning is
in the term config,

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:10.940
it generates that are used
to create DevOps project

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:13.360
and essential remote
state bucket configure

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.910
that specific deployments
to save the state locally

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:17.140
not remotely.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:20.110
The reason is because that
deployment specifically

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:24.010
is creating that DevOps project
and the remote state bucket

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.630
used to store the remote state.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:28.990
So if we haven't
created those resources,

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.160
how can we store
the state remotely?

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.640
So we have to do this
in a two-phased way.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.890
First, we set this
to true, meaning

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:39.250
disable the remote's
backend, so the state

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.500
will be saved locally.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.840
And then I'm going to
flip this to false.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.570
I'm going to regenerate
the Terraform configs

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:49.570
with the changed
config here, and that

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:51.280
will give me the new config.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.660
And that config will have the
state configure to remote.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:57.430
Then I can roll
on terraform init

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:02.320
to back up the state to
the remote state bucket.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.340
So a quick overview for what
I'm going to do in this demo.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.280
So I'm going to use Terraform
Engine and this config

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.800
to generate the
Terraform configs

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.800
and save them to a
local Git repository.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.980
And they will later be
checked into my private GitHub

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.649
repository, which will
be the source of truth

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:26.920
for the Terraform configs for my
testing or demo infrastructure.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.500
Then I will manually
deploy the DevOps project

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:31.670
and the CI/CD resources.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.730
So later deployments
or modifications

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:36.370
to our infrastructure can be
made through pull requests

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:39.250
and auto-deployed by post
submit cloudbuild jobs

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:45.040
once the PR is approved,
tested, and merged.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.760
Then I will make a commit
to include all the configs,

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.570
send it as a PR for review,
let pre-submit test to run

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:53.500
and pass.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:56.680
I will merge the PR and let
post submit cloudbuild job

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.460
to kick in and auto-deploy
the rest of the resources that

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.230
were not deployed manually.

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:06.160
Those will include the audit
project, audit resources,

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.440
monitoring project, and
the Forseti resources.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.140
And a few things to
note, Terraform Engine

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.480
